// PARAM: --analysis sign
// The concrete result is fixed: foo returns the literal 2, so 0 < y holds.
//
// The combine writes the callee's return slot into the caller's destination
// through the destination's kind conversion, unconditionally. Here that
// conversion is int32 to int32 and the value it converts was produced at
// int32, so it cannot change anything -- but a Sign value carries no kind, so
// nothing at the conversion can see that, and Positive converts to Top.
// Interval answers [2,2] on the same program because its concretization is
// bounded and the conversion is provably the identity on it.
//
// It is worth being exact about why the conversion cannot just skip the work
// it can see is redundant. Answering Positive here would claim
// ik_norm int32 v >= 0 for every v in gamma of Positive, and gamma of a Sign
// value is unbounded, so v = 2^31 refutes it. The conversion is the identity
// only on values already in int32's range, and nothing carries that fact: an
// abstract cell does not know the kind it holds, and teval reads a variable
// without normalizing. So this is not an implementation shortcut that could
// be removed on its own -- it is the missing invariant. Slices A7-A9 of
// docs/VAR_ID_MIGRATION.md close it: the tagged cell and the kind-aware
// operations, not A6b's seed, which bounds Interval cells and does nothing
// for a Sign value.
int32 foo() { return 2 }
void main() {
  int32 y;
  y := foo();
  __voblint_check(0 < y) // UNKNOWN
}
